---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Security of this site"
  description="The hardening applied to this site itself: headers, CSP, security.txt, and accessibility conformance — documented as a control-justification writing sample."
>
  <article class="container prose">
    <h1>Security of this site</h1>
    <p>
      This page documents the hardening on the site you're reading, and why each control is
      here. It's meant to double as a writing sample: the same "control → justification"
      reasoning I'd use in an audit report, applied to something you can inspect yourself right
      now with your browser's dev tools.
    </p>

    <h2>1.0 Static output, minimal attack surface</h2>
    <p>
      The site is built by Astro to static HTML/CSS with zero client-side JavaScript by default.
      There is no server-side application, database, or API for this site — no request ever
      executes application logic, because there isn't any at request time. The only JavaScript
      shipped is two small, non-framework scripts (mobile navigation toggle, dark-mode toggle),
      each under a kilobyte, bundled as external files rather than inline.
    </p>

    <h2>2.0 Zero third-party requests</h2>
    <p>
      Fonts (Source Serif 4, IBM Plex Mono) are self-hosted from <code>@fontsource</code>
      packages bundled at build time, not loaded from Google Fonts or any other CDN. There's no
      analytics, no tag manager, no embedded widgets. Open the Network tab on any page of this
      site — every request resolves to this origin. That's a deliberate privacy and
      attack-surface decision, not an oversight: a third-party script is a third party you're
      trusting with every visitor's traffic.
    </p>

    <h2>3.0 Security headers</h2>
    <p>
      Set via <code>_headers</code> (Cloudflare Pages) and <code>netlify.toml</code> (Netlify) so
      they apply regardless of host:
    </p>

    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Header</th>
            <th scope="col">Value</th>
            <th scope="col">Why</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Content-Security-Policy</td>
            <td>
              <code>default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self'; frame-ancestors
              'none'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'</code>
            </td>
            <td>
              No <code>unsafe-inline</code> anywhere — achievable because the static/no-JS-by-
              default approach means every script and stylesheet is a same-origin file, not an
              inline blob. Blocks injected inline scripts outright, even if an XSS bug existed.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Strict-Transport-Security</td>
            <td><code>max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains; preload</code></td>
            <td>Forces HTTPS for two years, including subdomains, and is eligible for browser preload lists.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>X-Content-Type-Options</td>
            <td><code>nosniff</code></td>
            <td>Stops the browser from MIME-sniffing a response into a different, more dangerous content type.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>X-Frame-Options / frame-ancestors</td>
            <td><code>DENY</code> / <code>'none'</code></td>
            <td>Set both, for browsers that only honor one: this site can't be framed by anyone, which rules out clickjacking.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Referrer-Policy</td>
            <td><code>strict-origin-when-cross-origin</code></td>
            <td>Sends full referrer only to same-origin requests; cross-origin requests get origin only, not full path/query.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Permissions-Policy</td>
            <td>camera, microphone, geolocation, USB, payment, etc. all disabled</td>
            <td>None of these APIs are used, so every one is explicitly locked to <code>()</code> rather than left to default.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy / -Resource-Policy</td>
            <td><code>same-origin</code></td>
            <td>Isolates this site's browsing context and resources from other origins, closing some cross-origin side-channel classes.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>4.0 security.txt</h2>
    <p>
      Published at <a href="/.well-known/security.txt">/.well-known/security.txt</a> per
      <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9116" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">RFC 9116</a>,
      mirrored at <code>/security.txt</code> for scanners that check the legacy location, with a
      contact address and expiry date.
    </p>

    <h2>5.0 Scan targets</h2>
    <p>
      This site targets an A/A+ on
      <a href="https://securityheaders.com" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">securityheaders.com</a>
      and SSL Labs, and no mixed content or inline event handlers anywhere in the codebase.
      <span class="todo mono">TODO: paste actual scan results/screenshots once deployed.</span>
    </p>

    <h2>6.0 Accessibility (WCAG 2.1 AA)</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>4.5:1 minimum contrast on body text, in both light and dark themes.</li>
      <li>Visible keyboard focus rings on every interactive element (nav links, buttons, form controls).</li>
      <li>Semantic landmarks (<code>nav</code>, <code>main</code>, <code>footer</code>) and a skip-to-content link.</li>
      <li>Respects <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code> — the only motion on the site is a hover underline and a theme-change color transition, both disabled for users who request less motion.</li>
      <li>Respects <code>prefers-color-scheme</code>, with a manual override that persists via <code>localStorage</code>.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .todo {
    display: block;
    margin-top: var(--space-1);
    color: var(--fg-muted);
  }
</style>
